 will tell you that  what we've been told is that shopkeepers  restaurant owners business owners have  been told they need to keep the lights  off in the evening also modes of public  transportation some of them will be  suspended and we also got an  announcement by the mayor of Kiev in  which he said this is Vitali Klitschko  he asked residents in the capital not to  use major electrical appliances he said  said that even saving and reducing  electricity consumption in each  residence in each household is going to  help stabilize the national Energy  Systems Operations so pretty drastic  restrictions are being put in place  what's been the reaction from ukrainians  are they behind this  well Tom everybody we've been speaking  with in the neighborhoods that have been  impacted by power outages after either  missile strikes or drone strikes these  past few days have told us that they are  worried that it is going to get worse  and that they are preparing themselves  as best they can a couple of days ago we  went out to destinyansky District that's  east of the capital here it's one of  kiev's districts that was an area where  a power infrastructure was hit by  Russian missiles and those residents  told us that this was the second week in  a row that they had been impacted by  power outage is that being said they  were back online just hours later and  that's something that we've seen a lot  the past couple of weeks is typically  the power grid in those impacted areas  they're quick to repair them enough so  that energy isn't impacted that much now  there is concern that it is only going  to get worse we have also spoken to  Residents in other parts of the country  in which they said they don't think the  government has done enough to prepare  the cities and to prepare the citizens  and that more infrastructure needs to be  protected and the more measures need to  be put in place so that if there are  more power outages residents have access  to heated areas and they 